WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.499
Speaker 1: I want to start today with something
many of you have been writing to me about,

00:00:03.499 --> 00:00:08.790
and it's the question essentially, if Joe
Biden doesn't run for reelection, do Democrats

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:16.059
have any good candidates for 2024, particularly
to run against Donald Trump if he's the nominee

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:17.840
or Ron DeSantis, if he's the nominee?

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.510
Now, why are we contextualizing this with
Trump and DeSantis?

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:22.510
Well?

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:27.980
Because they're the only two pool polling
anything even remotely viable in every CPAC

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.570
straw poll and Republican opinion poll.

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.680
It's Trump leading.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:37.660
And then Ron DeSantis with between 10 and
22 percent, depending on the poll or straw

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.329
poll that you look at.

00:00:39.329 --> 00:00:41.470
Everybody else is in the low single digits.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:47.430
So at least for now, let's theorize that it
will be or is likely to be Donald Trump or

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:49.520
Ron DeSantis in 2024.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:55.130
Now, I've already said before, it's unclear
if Joe Biden is going to run for reelection.

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:58.440
The language being used by the White House
is very narrow.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:05.180
When Jen Psaki is asked questions like if
Joe Biden plans to run, why hasn't he announced

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:11.030
or filed or whatever, and she'll say Joe Biden
has the intention of running for reelection,

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:13.570
which is a very specifically worded phrase.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:17.200
I think they just don't know if Joe Biden
is going to run for reelection or they may

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.500
know he's not going to.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.909
And then the question becomes, well, well,
so then what?

00:01:22.909 --> 00:01:27.080
And much of this is wrapped up with how the
midterm elections go.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:32.380
Much of this connects to the continued and
obvious efforts that the right is going to

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:37.400
make to take the election, whether they win
it or not.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:40.670
And unfortunately, it's not looking very good.

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:46.420
I think it would be an obvious lie if I said
to you, there's a great bullpen right now,

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:48.200
there is all sorts of great people.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:53.670
First of all, if Biden does run for reelection
against Trump, I don't see that going well

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:54.670
for Biden.

00:01:54.670 --> 00:02:00.040
And I've explained before that one of the
reasons for that is so far Biden's presidency.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:05.050
Although there is no doubt that there have
been a number of tangible successes which

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:08.649
I outlined last week, maybe the week before.

00:02:08.649 --> 00:02:15.130
It has not been an interesting presidency
in the sense of inspiring people.

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:21.300
Joe Biden's approval has been trickling down,
and it seems to me that Donald Trump, if than

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:26.620
if he was the nominee against Joe Biden, would
be able to sort of reinvigorate a lot of the

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:31.170
same people on whose vote he won in 2016.

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:33.840
I don't see that going particularly well.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:40.160
I am unsure about Biden versus DeSantis, but
unfortunately, DeSantis is almost like a more

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.430
competent Trump, which can be very dangerous.

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:46.220
So then the immediate conversation becomes,
if not Biden.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:47.410
What about Kamala Harris?

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:54.200
It would be natural in some sense that there
would sort of be a primary, but really that

00:02:54.200 --> 00:03:02.410
if it's not Biden, the DNC would kind of anoint
Kamala Harris as the de facto nominee.

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:04.379
That seems very problematic.

00:03:04.379 --> 00:03:11.620
Kamala Harris is current approval or favorability
is 39 percent, and that's lower than every

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:17.620
recent vice president and some by a lot at
this point in time.

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:20.709
This was taken at day 378.

00:03:20.709 --> 00:03:26.150
Mike Pence was more popular and more liked
than Kamala Harris.

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:30.450
Joe Biden was more popular and more liked
than Kamala Harris.

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:37.680
As Vice President, Dick Cheney was significantly
more favorable, favorably seen than Kamala

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:42.909
Harris, and Al Gore was dramatically more
favorably seen than Kamala Harris.

00:03:42.909 --> 00:03:48.940
So the numbers don't look great for Kamala
Harris right now.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:55.690
It looks like a very uphill battle if it's
going to be Harris versus Trump or Harris

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:58.770
versus Ron DeSantis, so that doesn't look
good.

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:01.159
Then there was this idea of bring Hillary
Clinton back.

00:04:01.159 --> 00:04:02.420
Now I already talked about why.

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:04.330
I think that's not a good idea.

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:10.250
I think it can't possibly go well, and there
are a number of different reasons.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:18.630
One is that Hillary Clinton never really inspired
the type of energy that I think is necessary,

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:22.400
particularly running against a reinvigorated
Trumpian electorate.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:25.770
And even if it's DeSantis, it will still be
a Trumpian electorate.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:29.599
DeSantis is a sort of Trump, although now
they are publicly feuding.

00:04:29.599 --> 00:04:30.860
Trump and DeSantis are.

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:33.470
The electorate is quite similar.

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:36.129
So then you've got the people saying, Well,
Bernie, Bernie is the guy.

00:04:36.129 --> 00:04:38.330
It's finally Bernie's time.

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:41.629
I'm not going to blow smoke anywhere.

00:04:41.629 --> 00:04:43.830
To me, Bernie's time is done.

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:46.379
Bernie ran twice.

00:04:46.379 --> 00:04:50.289
He would be 83 at the time of inauguration.

00:04:50.289 --> 00:04:55.719
If the idea is that there needs to be more
energy than Biden, although I agree that Bernie

00:04:55.719 --> 00:05:00.360
has more energy than Biden, it's not seeming
to me.

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:08.120
Like the guy who already lost twice is going
to be the guy who at 83 is going to come in

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:11.979
and, you know, blow the top off the electorate
and defeat Trump or DeSantis.

00:05:11.979 --> 00:05:15.900
If you have evidence and data that suggests
it could be Bernie.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:16.900
Let me know.

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:19.849
I'm willing to be told I'm wrong, but it's
not seeming great.

00:05:19.849 --> 00:05:21.289
Then you've got the AOC crowd.

00:05:21.289 --> 00:05:23.379
People say AOC, AOC has the energy.

00:05:23.379 --> 00:05:24.419
She has the likability.

00:05:24.419 --> 00:05:25.639
That's true.

00:05:25.639 --> 00:05:27.270
AOC has a lot of energy.

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:33.240
AOC would be old enough to be president by
two months, roughly two and a half months,

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:36.419
something like that at the time of the next
inauguration.

00:05:36.419 --> 00:05:46.029
It's not seeming plausible to me that 34 year
old AOC successfully runs on the basis that

00:05:46.029 --> 00:05:50.240
I'll be old enough by two months on Inauguration
Day.

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:54.659
That doesn't seem to me like the recipe for
a success.

00:05:54.659 --> 00:05:56.559
I'm just being realistic with you.

00:05:56.559 --> 00:05:58.680
I read something about Amy Klobuchar.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:00.999
Tons of people hate Amy Klobuchar.

00:06:00.999 --> 00:06:04.619
She never polled more than six in 2020.

00:06:04.619 --> 00:06:10.429
But I have to tell you, Klobuchar, to me,
sounds less crazy than Harris or Hillary in

00:06:10.429 --> 00:06:12.090
the context of 2024.

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:17.490
Now, Perry Bacon Jr. wrote a piece in The
Washington Post talking about, among other

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:24.139
people, Kentucky Gov. Andy Beshear, a white
male Democrat but from the South and the governor,

00:06:24.139 --> 00:06:29.660
rather than a senator, which sometimes can
be more appealing to people in different parts

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:31.999
of the country, the South, the Midwest, etc.

00:06:31.999 --> 00:06:33.569
It's an interesting idea.

00:06:33.569 --> 00:06:38.270
I don't know much about Andy Beshear, so I'll
sort of put a pin in that, but that's something

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:40.069
Perry Bacon Jr. wrote about.

00:06:40.069 --> 00:06:45.089
Now, Perry Bacon Jr. also writes about Tim
Kaine, who ran alongside Hillary in 2016.

00:06:45.089 --> 00:06:51.689
It is difficult to imagine a less exciting
and more milquetoast candidate than Tim Kaine.

00:06:51.689 --> 00:06:58.409
I feel almost embarrassed to even mention
the idea of Tim Kaine as the nominee.

00:06:58.409 --> 00:07:03.159
It's it's very tough to imagine that's the
direction, so I don't see any strong candidates

00:07:03.159 --> 00:07:04.159
right now.

00:07:04.159 --> 00:07:09.509
A few things I will mention on rhetoric, and
what I mean by that is in terms of being able

00:07:09.509 --> 00:07:15.189
to mix it up with people effectively in media
and in debates and come up with sort of like

00:07:15.189 --> 00:07:18.599
snappy comebacks and optics.

00:07:18.599 --> 00:07:24.020
There's something interesting about Pete Buttigieg,
but he's already suffered and been the target

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:30.300
of significant character assassination in
his role as secretary of Department of Transportation,

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:31.710
Secretary of Transportation.

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:37.189
You don't typically see that Secretary of
Transportation into presidential candidate.

00:07:37.189 --> 00:07:38.719
It's unclear what his fate would be.

00:07:38.719 --> 00:07:40.779
So I'm just not sure about it.

00:07:40.779 --> 00:07:44.559
And then it has to be said that on policy.

00:07:44.559 --> 00:07:50.149
Elizabeth Warren is one of the most left candidates
of anybody who's recently run other than Bernie.

00:07:50.149 --> 00:07:55.050
Her wealth tax proposal is something to the
left of what most Democratic candidates have

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:56.050
proposed.

00:07:56.050 --> 00:08:02.379
But on a personality personality level, the
country really does not seem to like Elizabeth

00:08:02.379 --> 00:08:05.939
Warren, so that doesn't seem particularly
good either.

00:08:05.939 --> 00:08:09.270
So it's a very tough situation.

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:14.330
Let's get through the midterms insofar as
what happens will inform what the dynamics

00:08:14.330 --> 00:08:16.750
of 2024 might be.

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:21.750
But I am not particularly optimistic about
the farm team right now.

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:24.389
I'm open to having my mind changed.

00:08:24.389 --> 00:08:49.440
Let me 

00:08:49.440 --> 00:09:04.769
know 

00:09:04.769 --> 00:09:15.100
what 

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:28.350
you think this this is.

